 "title": "Parametric Equations and Differential Equations",
 "seo_title": "Year 13 A Level Maths: Parametric and Differential Equations Worksheets",
 "short_title": "parametric and differential equations",
 "domain": "Pure Mathematics",
 "standard_code": "A level Mathematics: Pure mathematics",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/gce-as-and-a-level-mathematics",
 "standard_text": "Understand and use the parametric equations of curves and conversion between Cartesian and parametric forms; construct simple differential equations and solve first order differential equations with separable variables.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 13 A level maths worksheets on parametric equations (points, Cartesian equations and dy/dx) and first order differential equations with separable variables. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "To find a Cartesian equation, eliminate the parameter t.",
 "key_sub": "Separate the variables, then integrate both sides.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>Parametric equations give <i>x</i> and <i>y</i> in terms of a parameter <i>t</i>. To get a Cartesian equation, make <i>t</i> the subject of one equation and substitute into the other.</p><p>For d<i>y</i>/d<i>x</i> = f(<i>x</i>)g(<i>y</i>): ∫ 1/g(<i>y</i>) d<i>y</i> = ∫ f(<i>x</i>) d<i>x</i>. Use the given condition to find the constant.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'><i>x</i> = <i>t</i> + 1, <i>y</i> = <i>t</i>²</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>t</i> = <i>x</i> − 1</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>y</i> = (<i>x</i> − 1)²</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>d<i>y</i>/d<i>x</i> = 2<i>x</i>, <i>y</i> = 3 when <i>x</i> = 0</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>y</i> = <i>x</i>² + <i>c</i></p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>c</i> = 3</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>y</i> = <i>x</i>² + 3</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Forgetting the constant of integration.</li><li>Substituting the condition before integrating.</li><li>Dividing dx/dt by dy/dt.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do you turn parametric equations into a Cartesian equation?",
   "a": "Rearrange one equation to make the parameter the subject, then substitute into the other."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is a separable differential equation?",
   "a": "One that can be written as dy/dx = f(x)g(y), so the y terms and x terms can be put on different sides and integrated."
  }
 ],
